  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Uganda coffee communities: Promoting child education</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Child labour remains prevalent in Ugandan coffee farming communities, despite existing laws and policies aimed at addressing it. The underlying reasons for child labour are multifaceted. Research indicates that in these coffee-growing areas, factors contributing to child labour include poverty, detrimental cultural practices such as early marriage, insufficient or poor-quality educational facilities, unmotivated or absent teachers, illiteracy among parents or guardians, and a lack of awareness among caregivers.In the coffee regions of Greater Masaka and Rwenzori, the project adopted an area-based approach to establish Child Labour Free Zones (CLFZ). By fostering collaboration among stakeholders in the coffee sector, they jointly developed and implemented a program with the aim of eliminating all forms of child labour in the above regions.The project supported 3,000 coffee growing households in the Rwenzori region and 1,500 households in the Masaka region. The interventions included:1.	Mapping, monitoring, and addressing child labour through individual household visits.2.	Enrolling children in formal and vocational education, providing internships, and facilitating access to financing for small business startups.3.	Establishing Village Saving and Loan Associations to enhance household financial access.4.	Training households on Good Agricultural Practices to boost coffee farm income.5.	Conducting media campaigns and awareness activities about child labour and safe youth employment through social media, traditional media, and local leaders.6.	Organising project steering group meetings with all partners to share results, lessons learned, and establish a sustainable labour monitoring system.7.	Enhancing the performance of JDE&apos;s Due Diligence system by training staff on compliance and annually monitoring and reporting project results on the JDE website.As a result of the project implementation, coffee farmers in Rwenzori and Masaka have enhanced and improved capacities and livelihood strategies that promote their children&apos;s development and education, without involving them in labour. The project focused on addressing the root causes of child labour through increasing household income from coffee, and providing more children with access to education.</narrative>
  </description>
